    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "I think the answer is yes on all fronts. I think on day one, President Trump will re-institute probably over 90 executive orders that Biden removed. So during the first Trump administration, he had over 90 executive orders that did help shut down the border. We believe that Congress has given him a great deal of latitude. We've read the immigration law and we think he can go further than he's gone in the past. We believe that allowing migrants to come in for political asylum, the entire process is optional. So we think you don't have to begin the process. We believe that the president is the power when people are in the middle of the river or getting out of the river simply to put them back on the Mexican side. And that's what I'm going to be advocating. I'm going to have a hearing on this before the president is sworn in. It's going to be called Remain in Mexico. And we will have this hearing. And we're going to encourage him to interpret are migration laws to say that if you come in illegally you're ineligible for migration or for asylum and then you don't have to go through this year's long process of appeals now the people have already come i believe they can be uh... their parole can be revoked sort of like a criminal proceeding they're out on parole pending a trial well the trials you know happen in four years you can never find them and nobody forces it If Trump comes in and says, well, 2 million young adult men came in last year and were worried with their criminals, we're going to revoke their parole and immediately have their trials. I think you can go forward with that. People say there aren't enough judges. I think every border agent can be deputized to judge the case on whether or not someone is validly in the country. And so, no, I think a lot of this can be reversed. And it's just a matter of willpower. Biden had no willpower. I think Trump comes in with a mandate on the border. And I think you will see action.",
