  {
    "episode_id": 94485,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94485.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "And that is a real rub, and there is a real debate to be had there. You know, Anthony Vauci's conclusion was that even if an accident should occur, the knowledge gained would be worth the risk. And yet many other scientists, credentialed scientists with, you know, 20 years in academia and a couple hundred peer-reviewed papers say that they don't see any useful knowledge has been gained. It's sort of a misplaced curiosity. It's like, Hey guys, Ebola is really deadly. It's a 50% deadly virus. Why don't we mutate it and see if we can get it spread through the air to mammals? I know. And it's like, well, that's sort of a morbid teenage curiosity that some adults should look and say, well, gosh, what if it escaped the lab and killed everybody? You know, might be a good idea. And yet that's the kind of thing that Anthony Vauci approved because he had a very naive understanding of the dangers of this research. And there are many people who believe this is going to happen again. And so, you know, I'm going to do everything I can. I have a bill to set up a Presidential Commission to oversee this research such that if it's dangerous, this commission will have the ability to stop it. So I'm going to try to get that passed. We're trying to get it passed this year. If we don't, we'll try to get it and get the help of President Trump next year.",
